         events are in task_loop, which is the IDLE AP rather than the stuck
         BSP; the two stack pointers differing by exactly 0x1000 were two CPUs.
         And -smp 1 does not isolate the BSP -- this guest requires cpu>=2 and
         fails ACPI honestly, exit 241. Filtering events by address against the
         map is what separated the processors."}
 {:at "2026-09-09"
  :profile :release-gpt-image-hang-no-longer-reproduces
  :note "No coverage change. The wait that hangs tests
         `used->index == target` -- exact equality on a counter that only
         advances -- while the IRQ half of the same condition uses >=. If the
         used index ever passes target the loop can never succeed, and it keeps
         taking the device's MSI-X for a completion it has already missed. That
         is a hang rather than a delay, and the two want different treatment.
         virtio_blk_sector_io now detects exactly that condition -- used index
         past target, which no future completion can undo -- and returns with
         the numbers instead of spinning until the harness gives up. It is a
         LOGICAL bound, not a timing one: no threshold to tune and nothing that
         fires on a slow but healthy device.
         Measured after: the ESP profile is unaffected, VIRTIO_BLK_OK and exit
         0. The release image stopped hanging -- seven runs, all reaching the
         same deterministic stop, the already-explained input one.
         STATED CAREFULLY: the overshoot detector did NOT fire. So the cause is
         not confirmed. Either the check catches nothing and its presence
         changed timing enough to hide the race, or the hang has another source
         that seven runs did not sample. What is established is that the boot
         became deterministic where it was not, and what is not established is
         why."}
 {:at "2026-09-09"
  :profile :blk-wait-measured-on-the-healthy-path
  :note "A negative result, and it is against my own hypothesis.
         The overshoot theory needed the used index to have room to run ahead
         of target. Measuring the SUCCESS path says it does not: on both
         profiles the slowest wait that ever succeeded took ONE iteration, with
         used equal to target -- 49 requests on the build, 55 on the release
         image. A completion that is seen on the first look cannot arrive twice
         before anyone checks.
         Combined with the overshoot detector never firing across nine runs, of
         which zero hung, the honest position is: the hang no longer reproduces
         and nothing here explains why it did. The obvious candidate -- that
         adding the check fixed it -- is not supported: it never triggered. The
         likelier story is that some other change between those builds moved the
         timing, since several landed in between, and that is a story rather
         than a measurement.
         The check stays on its own merits. An exact-equality wait on a
         monotone counter beside an inequality on the counter next to it is
         fragile whether or not it is what bit us, and now it fails with
         numbers instead of spinning. But the node is NOT closed and the cause
         is NOT known, and the difference between those and a green run is the
         whole point of keeping this ledger."}
